{"id": "q0046", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 644994425, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "923cbbdfe3213927dfa36b1f8e0eb48a30d641d9b9bf7cf25d16765340d88836", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 3, "query": "Tim wanted to make lemonade for a pool party.  For a gallon of lemonade, his recipe called for 1 cup of fresh lemon juice.  He found that 6 lemons would yield 1 cup of juice.  He figured he would need to make 4 gallons of lemonade for the party.  His best friend Allen asked if Tim could make an extra gallon for him that was twice as tart as the other gallons.  How many lemons will Tim need?\n\nShow your work and end with the final numeric answer.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0046.wav", "input_sha256": "32303a4d2647ea70e6aa653b6863131143cb196357ca08a522c9ca79b95a6459", "input_samples": 453200, "input_s": 28.325, "n_real_chunks": 29, "score": 0.4149, "act": 0.9992, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "So, Tim wants to make four gallon s of lemonade for the party. Each gal lon requires one cup of lemon juice, so that means he needs four cups total for those four gallons. But then his friend Alan asked for an extra gallon that's twice as tart. That means double the lemon juice, so two cups for that extra gallon. Adding them up, four plus two gives us six cups of lemon juice in total. Now, since six lemons make one cup, we just multiply six lemons by six cups. Six times six is thirty- six. So Tim will need thirty- six lemons.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 28, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 35, "feed_lag_